when you start learning a new language  have you ever done the following you've  gone to the local bookstore and bought a  bunch of language learning materials you  have started surfing on the internet  trying to find every sort of language  platform you could find you have  downloaded a bunch of apps on your phone  well don't do that ever again hi my name  is Luca from Luke Alan Perez comm and  today we're going to talk about how to  choose that one language learning  resource that is going to make all the  difference  have you ever heard of the paradox of  choice  well the paradox of choice is when we  have so much that we cannot make a  decision that happens to me every day  for example when I try to watch Netflix  that is I start looking for something to  look for and there's so much stuff that  I end up spending more time looking for  things to watch than actually watching  this stuff this has happened a number of  times and the only solution is to think  beforehand before hopping on Netflix but  the problem still stands and it still  stands for a number of things language  learning included I still remember that  some 25 years ago when I started  learning German I just found a course OD  Agostini teach the disco birthday and  that's what I used for one year and a  half before moving on to another  resource so I beg you if you have just  started learning a language or if you  want to start learning a language make  sure that you're going to choose one and  only one resource and use it in order to  build a very solid foundation in your  target language ok now that you know  that you have to use just one resource  how do you go about choosing that one  resource well I think there are three  main features to factor in when it comes  to choosing a good language learning  material first your language learning  material has to be comprehensible what  does it mean that a language learning  resource is comprehensible  well it's comprehensible when its  content allows you to understand what  you're reading and what you're listening  to  so only one hand the content should  contain obviously the target language  but possibly it could also contain  another version the same version in your  native language or in a language you  speak well so that you can use that  language you can leverage that knowledge  in order to understand and dissect your  target language but the resource doesn't  necessarily have to be just bilingual it  could be monolingual but if that is the  case  it has to be extremely clear and it has  to explain things so that you can  understand it in that target language  and third there could be a resource that  has content mainly in your target  language but also a vocab section in  your own native language or some  explanation in your own native language  of its grammar features the second  factor is that that language resource  should be enjoyable if it's an app  whether it's an app a book or a website  you should like its content visually you  should like spending time looking at  that resource in terms of visual content  and on the other side you should like  spending time listening to the audio of  that resource clear slow audio is very  important at the very beginning and  third that resource that you're using  should be useful that is the content  should be aimed at the skill you want to  develop so for example if that language  learning resource had real everyday  dialogues in your target language that's  great if you want to speak that language  well and apply it to everyday life  instead of say having very long  descriptive texts of the geography of  the country where your target language  is spoken or history or other things  that you can actually do and you can  learn a little bit later down the line  imagine a hammer with December you can  kill someone or you can build something  you can kill or you can create no matter  the quality of the hammer it's what you  ultimately do with it that makes a  difference a language running a material  is a little bit like that hammer most  language learning materials are  relatively good in terms of content but  ultimately the real good language  learners know how to use these resources  nowadays most language learning  resources are actually already pretty  good but what really makes a difference  is how a learner uses them now get this  the education system all around the  world over the last 200 years has been  hinging on the acquisition of knowledge  instead and to the detriment of learning  a skill this endemic form of poor  training applies to a lot of fields  ranging from mathematics to physics and  obviously to language learning have you  ever wondered why after majoring in say  Spanish for five years you have realized  that you can't actually string a  sentence together in that language even  if you know a lot of words come to think  of it language learning is a combination  of two very important skills the  capacity of acquiring knowledge on the  one hand that is words and grammar rules  and the capacity of putting these words  together and that is forming correct  sentences in terms of grammar and  pronunciation now kids do that very well  look at a five-year-old kid they can  already form perfectly grammatical  sentences in their own language but if  you ask them what is an adverb what is  an adjective  they have no clue because they have  learned that at school after learning to  put together sentences adults to have  these two systems in place but they only  use one system and that is how we  acquire knowledge adults also have  exactly the same capacity that kids do  how do kids learn languages so well  without even knowing of  Remer well they combine knowledge a  little bit of knowledge they know 500 to  1000 words a relatively short a  relatively small amount of vocab but  they immediately train in forming  sentences in combining these words  together to form meaning and Alice do  not do that in formal education we  mostly focus on acquiring knowledge we  spend more time speaking about the  language speaking about it features of  Strabo patterns its pronunciation  patterns then actually speak in it so  whatever resource you're going to use  make sure that you don't read it as if  you were a normal book use it as a  manual to build skills just to give you  an example instead of making lists of  words and trying to memorize them make  sure that immediately you take these  words in pairs or in groups of three and  you combine them in order to create  short and meaningful sentences until the  age of 14 I had been shamed to this  educational system and I thought that  the best way to learn German was  actually to try to memorize lists of  words and learn its grammar patterns but  after struggling a few weeks with very  very boring grammar exercises I realized  that I could actually do something else  so I started using a translation method  translating from German into my native  language Italian and back a few days  later in order to understand the  patterns of that language that worked so  well that I used that to learn German  and after one year I started learning  Spanish and then I applied it to pretty  much every other language that I speak  right now so to wrap up the content of  the video first make sure that you spend  some time doing some proper research to  find the one resource to start with and  use that one resource for a few months  before moving onto another resource  second make sure that this resource is  comprehensible enjoyable and useful that  is aimed at developing the skill you  want to  develop from the very beginning and  third make sure that you learn how to  use that resource to build skills and  sub-skills within the realm of language  learning you should not leave through  that resource you should not just read  or listen to that resource you should do  things that are aimed at developing  skills putting words together learning  to up their entire sentences and  ultimately to speak the language so  there you have it if you want to know  more please click on the link in the  description box there's a deluge of  additional information about this  fantastic topic thank you very much and  I'll see you very soon here on YouTube  [Music]  you  [Music]  you

